---
import InnerPage from "../layouts/InnerPage.astro";

/* THE PAGE FOR THE SMALLEST NUMBER IN THE RESEARCH, AND THE BEST-QUALIFIED ONE.
 *
 * "twitch vod track obs" is 110 US searches a month at KD 0 (DataForSEO, 16
 * August 2026). That will not move sessions and this page is not built for
 * sessions. Everyone typing it has already worked out that they want the
 * broadcast and the archive to carry different audio, which is the exact problem
 * this product solves better than the alternatives. A visitor who arrives
 * understanding the problem is worth more than ten who arrive at a head term.
 * That is the argument; it is a conversion argument, not a traffic one.
 *
 * EVERY CLAIM HERE IS BOUNDED BY docs/internal/COPY-CONSTRAINTS.md, which was
 * written before any of this copy and exists because each trap below is a
 * sentence a reasonable person would write and would be wrong:
 *
 *   §1  The second track is a TWITCH ENHANCED BROADCASTING capability. Nothing
 *       here has measured YouTube, Kick, Facebook or Rumble accepting one, and
 *       internal/db/destinations.go refuses `-c:a copy` on RTMP for that reason.
 *       Do not let this page imply otherwise.
 *   §3  It needs a GPU, and that must appear IN THE SAME BLOCK that announces
 *       the feature -- not a footnote. This audience self-hosts on VPSes without
 *       one, and finding out after installing is the experience the caveat
 *       exists to prevent.
 *   §4  Do NOT write "safe". Do not promise a strike will not happen. Do not
 *       give legal advice. Describe the mechanism and let the reader conclude.
 *       The sanctioned phrasing is reproduced almost verbatim below.
 *
 *       THE H1 BREACHED THIS AND WAS CAUGHT BY AUDIT, not by writing. It read
 *       "The broadcast keeps the music. The archive does not" -- a statement
 *       about what TWITCH archives, which is not ours to make, in the largest
 *       type on the page, while the body four lines down had the sanctioned
 *       mechanism phrasing correct. Getting a rule right in the prose and wrong
 *       in the headline is the specific way this one fails.
 *   §6  Two safety properties are worth stating because this audience checks for
 *       them and rarely gets told.
 *
 * §2 is why the "no video ladder required" line is here at all: a reader assumes
 * multitrack VIDEO is a precondition, because the Twitch feature is named for
 * it. maximum_video_tracks: 1 returns one rendition and both audio tracks, which
 * is what makes this reachable for a project that stream-copies video.
 */
const facts = [
  {
    h: "One ingest, one encode, one connection",
    p: "The live broadcast carries the music. The VOD track does not. polyemesis builds both mixes from the tracks already arriving on your single SRT ingest, and sends them to Twitch over the same connection: your encoder uploads once and encodes video once.",
  },
  {
    h: "No video ladder required",
    p: "Enhanced Broadcasting is named for multitrack video, so the reasonable assumption is that you must send several video renditions to get a second audio track. You do not. Requesting a single video track returns one rendition and both audio tracks, which is what makes this reachable at all for a server that copies video through untouched.",
  },
  {
    h: "A second mix cannot break your broadcast",
    p: "If the secondary mix will not compile, it is a warning and the stream goes out without it. An optional VOD track must never veto a working broadcast. A primary mix that will not compile is still an error, because that one is the broadcast.",
  },
  {
    h: "Adding it does not rewrite what you already send",
    p: "A destination that gains a VOD track keeps its live mix byte-for-byte identical to what it produced before. Turning the feature on is additive, and turning it off returns you exactly where you were.",
  },
];

const index = [
  { id: "before-you-install", label: "Before you install anything" },
  { id: "what-it-actually-does", label: "What it actually does" },
  { id: "what-this-page-is-not-telling-you", label: "What this page is not telling you" },
];
---

<InnerPage
  longform
  title="Twitch VOD track: different audio live and on the archive"
  description="Send Twitch a live mix with the music and a VOD mix without it, from one SRT ingest and one video encode. What it needs, and what it does not."
  path="/twitch-vod-track"
  crumbs={[
    { label: "polyemesis", href: "/" },
    { label: "Multistream", href: "/multistream" },
    { label: "Twitch VOD track" },
  ]}
  heading="The broadcast carries the music. The VOD track does not."
  lede="Twitch can carry a second audio track alongside the live one, and use it for the VOD. polyemesis builds that second mix from the same upload, so what your viewers hear live and what stays on the archive can differ, without a second encoder, a second upload, or a second stream key."
  index={index}
  band={{ heading: "Two mixes from one upload.", secondary: { label: "How the mix matrix works", href: "/docs/audio-routing" } }}
>
  {/* §3: THE GPU CAVEAT IS IN THE BLOCK THAT ANNOUNCES THE FEATURE. It is not
      a footnote, it is not further down the page, and it must not be moved.
      The reader most likely to want this runs a headless VPS. */}
  <div id="before-you-install" class="panel lf-callout">
    <p class="lf-label">Before you install anything</p>
    <p>
      This is a <strong>Twitch Enhanced Broadcasting</strong> capability, and
      Twitch requires a GPU. A host that reports none is refused the
      negotiation outright, so on a headless VPS, which is where a lot of
      self-hosted streaming lives, this particular feature will not come up.
      Everything else polyemesis does works there; this one does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is also Twitch-only. Nothing in this project has measured YouTube,
      Kick, Facebook or Rumble accepting a second audio track, and the code
      declines to send one to them rather than guessing.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing worth knowing if you came here from OBS's own Twitch VOD
      track: that mechanism tags a second track with FLV metadata, and
      obsproject/obs-studio issue #13636 (read 15 August 2026) reports it
      conflicting with Enhanced Broadcasting, with live audio archived
      regardless of the track exclusions. polyemesis does not use that path;
      it negotiates the second track through Enhanced Broadcasting itself.
      What Twitch finally archives is still Twitch's decision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="what-it-actually-does">
    <h2>What it actually does</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      {facts.map((f) => (
        <article class="lf-split">
          <h3>{f.h}</h3>
          <p>{f.p}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="what-this-page-is-not-telling-you">
    <h2>What this page is not telling you</h2>
    <p>
      polyemesis builds a second mix and sends it. What Twitch does with it, and
      whether any particular rights-holder agrees with the result, is not
      something this project can promise and nothing here should be read as legal
      advice. The mechanism is described above; the conclusion is yours to draw.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same mechanism has nothing to do with Twitch in the general case. A
      second mix is only the two-destination version of what this server does
      everywhere: sum the tracks you choose, per destination, from one upload.
      The archive is simply the most common reason to want two.
    </p>
    <ul class="lf-links">
      <li><a href="/docs/audio-routing">How the mix matrix works</a></li>
      <li><a href="/multistream">Multistreaming, three ways</a></li>
      <li><a href="/download">Install it</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</InnerPage>
